<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto-William, Count of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 958–1026</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto-William (c. 958–1026) was the first Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and founder of the comital house of the Free County. A son of Adalbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and grandson of King Berengar II, he was adopted by his stepfather Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a> and after 1002 contested the duchy, unsuccessfully, with King Robert II. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a> and gave Nevers to his son-in-law Landry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-william-count-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Otto-William, Count of Burgundy" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Faustin Poey d'Avant (1792–1864) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OtWilBur03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
